{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"mutual-timid-gypsymoth","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# A code-review criterion for comment/docstring VERBOSITY in source files\n\n## Why — a concrete, dated occurrence rather than a style preference\n\nWorking ticket 39c1 (epic e369), the DC transport's Epic Link fix pushed\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` to **822 lines against the locked 800 cap**. The overage was\nalmost entirely PROSE, not code: the implementation itself was roughly size-neutral (it replaced one\nwrite path with another), but the docstrings and inline comments explaining it were not. Getting\nunder the cap took four successive trimming passes — 822 → 810 → 806 → 799 → 795 — and by the third\npass the work had become \"shorten sentences until the number is small enough\", which is precisely\nwhat `AGENTS.md` warns against when it says never to split \"mechanically to hit a number\".\n\nTwo of those passes were legitimate (removing a comment describing an option that no longer existed,\nand deleting a paragraph the harness had just PROVEN FALSE). The other two were pure compression of\naccurate prose. That asymmetry is the signal: **there was no review criterion that would have caught\nthe verbosity when it was written, so it was only caught by a size gate, at the worst possible\nmoment — during a fix, under time pressure, by the author of the prose.**\n\n## The failure mode this criterion should name\n\nrebar's codebase deliberately writes explanatory comments — the \"why\", the bug number, the failure\nmode a guard prevents. That is a genuine strength and this criterion **must not attack it**. The\npathology is narrower and distinguishable:\n\n * **Restating the code in prose.** A docstring that says what the next three lines plainly say.\n * **Duplicated rationale.** The same explanation in the module docstring, the method docstring, AND\n   an inline comment — so a correction has three sites and, in practice, gets applied to one. This\n   file had the greenhopper mechanism explained in all three places; when the harness refuted it, two\n   of the three were still asserting the false claim, and one directly contradicted another ten lines\n   below it.\n * **Narrative that belongs on the ticket.** Multi-paragraph history of what was tried and rejected.\n   Valuable — but its durable home is the ticket and the commit message, both of which are searchable\n   and none of which count against a module-size cap.\n * **Comment-to-code ratio inside a single unit** far above the file's own norm.\n\n## What makes this reviewable rather than bikesheddable\n\nThe criterion should be **comparative, not absolute** — flag a unit whose comment density is a large\nmultiple of its own file's or package's median, rather than asserting a global ratio. And it should\nrequire a SPECIFIC relocation, not \"shorten this\": name the duplicated site to delete, or the ticket\nthe narrative belongs on. A finding that says \"this comment is too long\" is not actionable; one that\nsays \"this paragraph repeats the module docstring at :24-29, delete one\" is.\n\nStrong candidate for **advisory rather than blocking**, at least initially. Blocking would risk the\nreviewer stripping the explanatory comments that make this codebase navigable — the opposite of the\nintent — and the existing module-size gate already provides the hard backstop.\n\n## Open questions for the design pass\n\n * Does it belong as a plan-review criterion, a code-review criterion, or both? The occurrence above\n   was caught at code-write time, which argues for code review (`rebar review-code`).\n * Should it be scoped to files already near the size cap, where the cost is concrete, rather than\n   applied everywhere? That would make it cheap and would target the real harm.\n * Is there an existing criterion it should extend rather than duplicate? The generated registry\n   (`docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md`) should be checked first — adding a near-duplicate criterion\n   would itself be an instance of the redundancy this one is about.\n * How does it avoid punishing the load-bearing comments this repo requires (the bug-number\n   attributions, the \"why this guard exists\" notes, the recorded refutations)? A criterion that\n   cannot tell those from padding would do net harm and should not ship.","id":"95c1-f6eb-0fa9-402e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"status":"idea","tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Code-review criterion: flag overly verbose comments and docstrings in source files"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785784342028358001,"uuid":"df7dbf15-6f3f-48a0-9c16-022c776088a6"}